ls - Lists files and directories in the current folder, including options for detailed or hidden files.

pwd - Prints the full path of the current working directory.

cd - Changes the current directory to the specified one; use cd .. to go up one level.

mkdir - Creates a new directory with the given name.

rmdir - Removes empty directories.

rm - Deletes files or directories; use -r for recursive and -f to force deletion.

cp - Copies files or directories; -r copies directories recursively.

mv - Moves or renames files and directories.

touch - Creates a new empty file or updates the timestamp of an existing file.

cat - Displays the contents of a file in the terminal.

head - Shows the first few lines of a file.

tail - Shows the last few lines of a file.

chmod - Changes file permissions such as read, write, and execute.

chown - Changes file ownership and group.

ps - Displays information about active processes.

kill - Sends a signal to terminate a process by its PID.

grep - Searches for a pattern or string within files or output.

find - Searches for files and directories matching specified criteria.

echo - Prints text or variable values to the terminal.

man - Displays the manual or help page for other commands.

clear - Clears the terminal screen.

sudo - Executes commands with superuser (root) privileges.

history - Displays a list of previously executed commands.

uname - Shows system information like the kernel and OS type.

df - Displays disk space usage for mounted file systems.

du - Shows the size of files and directories.

wget - Downloads files from the internet.

ssh - Connects securely to a remote machine using the SSH protocol.

tar - Archives and extracts files from tarballs (.tar).

top/htop - Shows dynamic real-time system process and resource usage.
